I wrote the fan review to `review-fan.json` in the Anthony Ippolito run folder. It passes the review schema, its hashes match the request, and every quoted passage appears exactly in the draft. I changed no other project files and published nothing; the only other file I wrote was a scratch copy of the claim list in `/tmp`.

**Four major findings.** Three of them leave out evidence that favors Type 3, so fixing them should narrow the 6-vs-3 margin rather than flip it.
- **fan-01, training timeline:** The draft says he changed his body "before anyone asked." Farrelly says the opposite on Collider, the source the draft itself cites: "after we got it… we had him working out for a year." A People interview, relayed by Generation Iron, also says training began after he was cast.
- **fan-02, wrong year:** The broken-hand Grand Army tape is dated 2020 in three places, but 2020 is when he gave the interviews. Baruch lists his baseball season as 2019, and Netflix ordered Grand Army in October 2019.
- **fan-03, trimmed quote:** The Six case quotes Farrelly's "He was so prepared, you know, and humble." It drops the sentences just before it in the same answer: "Very Stallone-like in his approach to this. He just made it happen."
- **fan-04, missing premise:** The article never says the film is about Stallone refusing to sell Rocky unless he played the lead. Without that, THR's "same relentless drive" quote has nothing to refer back to, and the obvious parallel to Ippolito's own unasked-for tape never gets weighed.

**Four minor findings:**
- **fan-05:** Ippolito's own account that Stallone and his wife were "really big fans" (via People) is missing from the "did Stallone like it" section and FAQ.
- **fan-06:** His childhood route from baseball TV spots to an agent is missing.
- **fan-07:** The Type 1 section doesn't say his Grand Army character is one of the boys in the assault storyline.
